<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dobrica Ćosić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/2014">2014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Dobrica Cosic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dobrica Ćosić was a Serbian novelist and political figure whose works on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s wars made him a leading voice of Serbian nationalism. In 1992 he became the first president of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, formed by Serbia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. He clashed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a> and was removed by the federal parliament in 1993.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dobrica-cosic.jpg" alt="Dobrica Ćosić" width="320" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was BoskoM at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%94%D0%BE%D0%B1%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%86%D0%B0_%D0%8B%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%B8%D1%9B_(Dobrica_Chosich),_firct_President_of_Federal_Republic_of_Yugoslavia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dobrica Ćosić was born Dobrosav Ćosić on 29 December 1921 in the village of Velika Drenova near Trstenik, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. He attended a vocational agriculture school in Valjevo and joined the communist youth organisation in Negotin in 1939. In 1941 he joined the communist Partisans, and after the liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in October 1944 he held communist leadership positions, including work in the Serbian republican Agitation and Propaganda commission. His first novel, Daleko je sunce (The Sun is Far Away), appeared in 1951, was a success and launched his literary career; by then he had stopped working professionally for the Communist Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1956 he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> for a meeting of literary magazine editors when the Hungarian revolt began, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 31 October on a plane carrying Yugoslav Red Cross aid. He wrote a detailed report that some think shaped the official Yugoslav view of the revolt. In late 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> and Edvard Kardelj chose him for the committee that drew up a new party programme, completed in 1958. In 1961 he joined Tito on a 72-day voyage on the yacht Galeb to eight non-aligned African countries.</p><p>Between 1961 and 1962 he argued with the Slovenian intellectual Dušan Pirjevec over autonomy, nationalism and centralism, favouring a stronger federal authority; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> supported his arguments, yet measures after 1962 followed Pirjevec's position. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> decentralised after 1963, Ćosić became convinced that the Serbian population was imperilled. In May 1968 he condemned the nationalities policy in a speech to the Central Committee of the Serbian League of Communists, objecting especially to greater autonomy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a>, and thereafter acted as a dissident. He was nominated for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>'s death Ćosić helped lead a movement for equality for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> within the Yugoslav federation. He was a member of the Serbian Academy of Sciences and Arts and endorsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a>'s leadership in 1989. In 1992 he became the first president of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, formed by Serbia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, and held the office until 1993. Liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> saw him as one of the key figures behind the Greater Serbia project. He later turned against Milošević and was removed from his post for that reason, and in 2000 he publicly joined the anti-Milošević organisation Otpor!. His books have been translated into 30 languages, and he twice won the NIN award for literature. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 18 May 2014, aged 92.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dobrica Ćosić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first president, <b>Dobrica Ćosić</b> (1992–1993), was forced out for opposing him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Velika Drenova, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.63,21.13&amp;year=1981" aria-label="Velika Drenova on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Dobrica Ćosić"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4453 3824h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4453 3824h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.63,21.13&amp;year=1981">What was here in 1981? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
